<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Scaling Assumption</div>
<h2>Why Does Frontier Reasoning Require Massive Scale?</h2>
<p>The field's prevailing answer is: because it does. Models capable of solving AIME competition problems — the hardest American math olympiad problems — have historically lived in the 100B–1T parameter range. DeepSeek V3.2 at 671B, Kimi K2.5 at 1T, GLM-5 at 744B. The implicit assumption is that complex mathematical reasoning requires vast parameter capacity to memorize patterns, chain long derivations, and recover from errors.</p>
<p>This assumption shapes everything: the hardware requirements, the training budgets, the deployment costs, and ultimately who has access to frontier reasoning ability. A 3B model solving competition math at the same level as a 671B model would be extraordinary — if it's even possible.</p>

<!-- Parameter efficiency canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">parameter-efficiency — AIME26 score vs. model size (log scale)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">VibeThinker-3B highlighted in purple</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="efficiency-canvas" style="width:100%;height:320px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The chart above plots every model from the VibeThinker-3B paper against its parameter count. The frontier band — where DeepSeek V3.2, GLM-5, Kimi K2.5 cluster — sits at 94–96 on AIME26. VibeThinker-3B at 3B parameters lands squarely in that band at 94.3. The purple dot isn't an outlier — it's inside the cluster, 200× smaller than its neighbors.</p>
<p>This is the empirical puzzle VibeThinker-3B sets out to explain. The answer, it turns out, lies in a distinction the field rarely makes explicit: <em>verifiable reasoning is a fundamentally different kind of capability than knowledge storage</em>.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-hypothesis" data-code="hypothesis">
<div class="section-no">02 — Two Kinds of Capability</div>
<h2>The Parametric Compression-Coverage Hypothesis</h2>
<p>VibeThinker-3B motivates its approach with a structural claim about how different capabilities are encoded in parameters. The paper calls it the <strong>Parametric Compression-Coverage Hypothesis</strong>:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">hypothesis</div>
  <span class="math-var">Verifiable Reasoning</span> → parameter-dense (compression problem)<br>
  <span class="math-var">Open-domain Knowledge</span> → parameter-expansive (coverage problem)<br><br>
  <span class="math-comment">
  Reasoning = search + constraint satisfaction + error correction<br>
  Knowledge = fact recall + semantic association + long-tail coverage
  </span>
</div>

<p>The key insight: mathematical problem-solving doesn't require you to have seen every theorem — it requires you to reliably execute a structured procedure: parse the problem, explore solution paths, check constraints, backtrack on errors. These operations are <em>algorithmic</em>, not encyclopedic. They can be distilled into a compact, reusable reasoning core.</p>
<p>Knowledge recall is different. To answer "what year was the Eiffel Tower built?" or "what's the molecular weight of caffeine?" you need to have stored those specific facts. Facts are long-tail. The more domains you want to cover, the more parameters you need — it's a coverage problem, not a compression problem.</p>

<!-- Two-panel canvas: Dense core vs. Sprawling coverage -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">reasoning vs. knowledge — how capabilities occupy parameter space</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a panel to focus</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="capability-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;cursor:pointer"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This explains the GPQA-Diamond gap. On that knowledge-heavy benchmark, VibeThinker-3B scores 70.2 — well below the 87–91 range of the largest models. The hypothesis predicts exactly this: you can compress reasoning into 3B parameters, but you cannot compress all human knowledge. The 3B model has a powerful reasoning engine but limited encyclopedic storage.</p>
<p>On verifiable tasks — math competitions, executable coding — where the goal is to execute a correct procedure rather than recall a specific fact, the 3B model can compete with systems 200× its size.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-pipeline" data-code="pipeline">
<div class="section-no">03 — Spectrum-to-Signal</div>
<h2>A Four-Stage Post-Training Pipeline</h2>
<p>VibeThinker-3B begins from Qwen2.5-Coder-3B (a 3B base model) and applies a staged post-training pipeline called <strong>Spectrum-to-Signal</strong>. The name captures the strategy: the SFT phase builds a broad <em>spectrum</em> of diverse reasoning approaches, and the RL phase then amplifies the correct <em>signals</em> within that spectrum.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="pipeline-steps">
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">SFT Stage 1 — Broad Coverage</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Train on math, code, STEM, chat, and instruction-following data. Diversity-Exploring Distillation generates multiple solution paths per problem (not just one best answer), building a wide candidate solution space for RL to explore.</div>
      <div class="step-code">data: math + code + STEM + chat + IF → 5 epochs</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">SFT Stage 2 — Hard Reasoning Focus</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Filter to problems where error rate &gt; 0.75 (using VibeThinker-1.5B with 8 rollouts) and reasoning traces &gt; 5K tokens. Shifts distribution toward long-horizon derivation and complex constraint satisfaction.</div>
      <div class="step-code">filter: error_rate &gt; 0.75, trace_len &gt; 5K → 2 more epochs</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Multi-Domain RL (Math → Code → STEM)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">MGPO reinforcement learning applied sequentially across domains. 64K context window throughout — no progressive expansion, which the paper found degrades long-horizon reasoning for a strong SFT checkpoint. Includes a Long2Short stage that rewards shorter correct solutions.</div>
      <div class="step-code">MGPO(math) → MGPO(code) → MGPO(STEM) at 64K ctx</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Offline Self-Distillation + Instruct RL</div>
      <div class="step-desc">High-quality trajectories from the Math/Code/STEM RL checkpoints are filtered by learning potential score (how surprising the trace is to the student model) and distilled back into a unified model. Instruct RL adds strict instruction-following on top.</div>
      <div class="step-code">distill(math_ckpt + code_ckpt + stem_ckpt) → instruct RL</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="pipeline-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="pipeline-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
  <button id="pipeline-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<p>One counterintuitive finding: earlier work used a progressive context window expansion strategy (short context first, then expand) to improve training efficiency. VibeThinker-3B tried this and found it <em>hurts</em> when the SFT checkpoint is already strong. The paper hypothesizes that high truncation disrupts existing high-quality long-horizon behaviors rather than just filtering noisy traces. So they use a single 64K context window from the start.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-mgpo" data-code="mgpo">
<div class="section-no">04 — Train at Your Limit</div>
<h2>MGPO: Focus RL on the Capability Boundary</h2>
<p>The RL algorithm powering VibeThinker-3B is <strong>MaxEnt-Guided Policy Optimization (MGPO)</strong>, introduced in their previous 1.5B work. The core insight is simple: not all training problems are equally useful for learning.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A problem the model solves correctly 100% of the time (p=1.0) — already saturated, zero gradient signal</li>
  <li>A problem the model never solves (p=0.0) — too hard, only noise in the gradient</li>
  <li>A problem the model solves ~50% of the time — <strong>exactly at the capability boundary</strong>, maximum learning signal</li>
</ul>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">MGPO weight formula</div>
  <span class="math-var">p(q)</span> = empirical accuracy on prompt q (across G rollouts)<br>
  <span class="math-var">w(q)</span> = exp(−γ · D<sub>ME</sub>(p(q) ‖ 0.5))<br><br>
  <span class="math-comment">
  D_ME measures distance from maximum-entropy point 0.5<br>
  γ controls sharpness: high γ → only use p≈0.5 prompts<br>
  Applied as weight to the standard GRPO clipped objective
  </span>
</div>

<p>MGPO implements this by computing an empirical accuracy p(q) for each prompt (how often the model gets it right across G sampled rollouts), then weighting the RL update by w(q) — a bell-shaped function peaked at p=0.5. The steepness is controlled by the γ hyperparameter.</p>

<!-- MGPO weight curve — interactive γ selector -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">MGPO weight w(q) as a function of empirical accuracy p(q)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">select γ to see sharpness</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="mgpo-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="ctrl-row" id="gamma-btns">
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-gamma="0.5">γ = 0.5 (flat)</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" data-gamma="2">γ = 2 (default)</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-gamma="5">γ = 5 (sharp)</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-gamma="10">γ = 10 (hard boundary)</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>With γ=2 (the default), MGPO still gives some weight to problems at p=0.2 or p=0.8, but concentrates gradient mass near p=0.5. With γ=10, only problems very close to 50% accuracy contribute meaningfully — the model trains almost exclusively on problems at its exact current capability edge.</p>
<p>This is inspired by the maximum-entropy principle: the p=0.5 point is where the model's uncertainty is highest, meaning it has the most to gain from additional training signal. Problems where the model is already certain (either direction) contribute little to entropy reduction.</p>

<h3>Long2Short Math RL</h3>
<p>After standard MGPO for accuracy, VibeThinker-3B applies a second Math RL phase targeting <em>efficiency</em>. Within each group of correct rollouts, it rewards shorter solutions more. The reward redistribution is zero-sum over the correct subset, so the mean reward is unchanged and the overall RL objective isn't perturbed — only the relative preference among correct answers shifts toward conciseness.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-clr" data-code="clr">
<div class="section-no">05 — Claim-Level Verification</div>
<h2>CLR: Test-Time Scaling via Reasoning Audits</h2>
<p>At inference time, VibeThinker-3B can optionally apply <strong>Claim-Level Reliability Assessment (CLR)</strong> — a test-time scaling strategy that improves accuracy without changing model parameters. The idea: instead of just taking the majority-vote answer across many rollouts, <em>also check whether the key logical steps in each rollout are actually correct</em>.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="clr-steps">
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Generate K = 32 candidate trajectories</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Sample K=32 full reasoning traces using the same temperature and top-p as standard evaluation. Each trace ends with a candidate answer.</div>
      <div class="step-code">traces = model.sample(problem, K=32, temp=1.0)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Extract M = 5 decision-relevant claims per trace</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each trajectory, identify the 5 key intermediate logical claims that most affect the final answer — the "load-bearing" reasoning steps, not just the conclusion.</div>
      <div class="step-code">claims[k] = extract_key_claims(traces[k], M=5)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Self-verify each claim</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The model acts as its own verifier, attempting to falsify or validate each claim. Output is a binary verdict v ∈ {0, 1} per claim per trajectory.</div>
      <div class="step-code">verdicts[k] = [model.verify(c) for c in claims[k]]</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Compute nonlinear reliability score r_k</div>
      <div class="step-desc">r_k = (mean of verdicts)^M. The M-th power is key: a trace with all 5 claims verified scores 1.0, but a trace with even one wrong claim scores much lower — penalizing flawed intermediate logic harshly.</div>
      <div class="step-code">r_k = (mean(verdicts[k])) ** M  # M=5</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Select reliability-weighted majority answer</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Group candidate answers by equivalence. Select the answer group maximizing the sum of reliability scores across all traces in that group.</div>
      <div class="step-code">best = argmax_G sum(r_k for k if answer[k] in G)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="clr-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="clr-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 5</span>
  <button id="clr-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<p>The nonlinear scoring function is the key design choice. A simple majority vote weights all traces equally — a trace with a subtle logical error in step 3 counts the same as a trace with all steps correct. CLR's reliability score r_k = (mean verdicts)^M means a trace with 4/5 claims verified scores only 0.33 (0.8^5), while a trace with 5/5 scores 1.0. Flawed-but-correct-final-answer traces are strongly downweighted.</p>
<p>CLR adds significant compute (32× the traces, plus verification passes), but requires no model updates and can be applied on top of any checkpoint. The gains are substantial: AIME26 improves from 94.3 → 97.1, HMMT25 from 89.3 → 95.4, and IMO-AnswerBench from 76.4 → 80.6.</p>
</section>

